<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#old-javanese">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Javanese</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Javanese, or Kawi, is the literary and administrative language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> recorded from the late 8th century, heavily influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>. Hundreds of royal charters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom">Medang</a> kingdom and later Javanese and Balinese states were written in it, as was a rich court poetry, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjunawiwaha">Arjunawiwaha</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-javanese.jpg" alt="Old Javanese" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raden Wijaya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_of_a_stone_stele_written_in_Kawi_script.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Old Javanese, also called Kawi, is an Austronesian language and the oldest attested phase of the Javanese language. It was natively spoken in the central and eastern parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, in what are now the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, Yogyakarta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. As a literary language it was used across Java and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>. Its most important shaping force was its Austronesian heritage in vocabulary, sentence structure and grammar, shared with its sister languages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> influence was deep, and it was almost exclusively Sanskrit: no other Indian language left traces, unlike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a>. P. J. Zoetmulder's Old Javanese-English Dictionary of 1982 has about 25,500 entries, no fewer than 12,500 of them borrowed from Sanskrit. In any given literary work about 25% of the vocabulary comes from Sanskrit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Old Javanese has six vowels and twenty consonants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> transliteration. Verbs are built with affixes that mark voice, aspect and mood: the active voice with the prefix (m)aN- or the infix -um-, the passive with the prefix ka- or the infix -in-. Verbs do not formally distinguish present and past time. In a basic clause the predicate comes first, the subject follows, and a particle, most commonly ta, separates them, as in lunghā ta sira, meaning he leaves.</p><p>It was written in the Kawi script, a Brahmic script related to the Pallava and Kadamba scripts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, from the 8th to the 16th century. Traditionally Kawi is written on lontar, prepared palm leaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Medieval poems in Old Javanese, called layang kawi or kakawin, continued to circulate in the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, Surakarta and Yogyakarta and were held in high regard. From the 18th century literature inspired by them was written in Modern Javanese. Kakawin works include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> of about 870, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-kanwa">Mpu Kanwa</a>'s Arjunawiwaha of about 1030, the Bharatayuddha of Mpu Sedah and Mpu Panuluh of 1157, and the Nagarakertagama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-prapanca">Mpu Prapanca</a> of 1365. Mpu Tantular's Kakawin Sutasoma gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> its national motto, Bhinneka Tunggal Ika, which is more correctly rendered as scattered, remaining as one.</p><p>Kawi is not truly extinct as a spoken language. It is used in wayang performances and in parts of Javanese weddings, such as the ceremonies of Peningsetan and Panggih. The first scholar to treat Kawi seriously was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-von-humboldt">Wilhelm von Humboldt</a>, who considered it the father of all Malay-Polynesian languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Old Javanese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court's patronage of Hindu-Buddhist traditions produced artistic and literary achievements, including the temples of Panataran and the brick architecture of Trowulan, and <b>Old Javanese</b> works such as the Nagarakretagama of Mpu Prapanca (1365), which describes the realm at its height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa ordered the Mahabharata translated into <b>Old Javanese</b> in 996.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Malay used the Pallava and <b>Kawi</b> scripts, known from inscription stones, and Rencong was also used.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>It was translated and retold across India and beyond: in Tamil from the 9th century, in the Javanese <b>Kawi</b> Mahabharata of the late 10th century, in Kannada by Pampa and Kumaravyasa, in the Telugu Andhra Mahabharatam of the 11th to 14th centuries, in Bengali, Odia, Assamese, Marathi, Punjabi…</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal charters in Old Balinese, later <b>Old Javanese</b>, show irrigation communities, ancestors of the subak system, and temples endowed with land.</blockquote><a href="/state/bedulu-kingdom">Bedulu Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Airlangga's Pucangan inscription of 1041 tells his story in Sanskrit and <b>Old Javanese</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li></ul></section>
